---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TheProblem from '../components/sections/TheProblem.astro';
import WhoThisIsFor from '../components/sections/WhoThisIsFor.astro';
import SeeItRun from '../components/sections/SeeItRun.astro';
import CustomerOutcomes from '../components/sections/CustomerOutcomes.astro';
import TheYield from '../components/sections/TheYield.astro';
import NutritionFacts from '../components/sections/NutritionFacts.astro';
import PaperIn from '../components/sections/PaperIn.astro';
import Sage from '../components/sections/Sage.astro';
import TheOtherTools from '../components/sections/TheOtherTools.astro';
import BuiltForKitchens from '../components/sections/BuiltForKitchens.astro';
import StartHere from '../components/sections/StartHere.astro';
import StickyCta from '../components/StickyCta.astro';
---

<!--
  Ten scroll stops, one claim each.

  SeeItRun moved from fourth to third on 2026-08-21. SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT at
  390x844, so nobody has to re-litigate it:

    What it cost. TheProblem's first pain and its answer in CustomerOutcomes
    sat 942px apart before (1.12 viewports); they now sit 1,546px apart (1.83).
    The move cost 604px, or 0.72 viewports, of recall distance.

    What it bought. The proof video moved from 3,155px to 1,897px: 3.7 mobile
    viewports down to 2.2, a gain of 1.5.

  The pairing reads as 1:1 on paper, but at 390px it was never co-visible: at
  1.12 viewports the reader has already scrolled a full screen past "you quote
  from an old price" before "bring the real price back" appears. So the move
  made a non-adjacency somewhat worse rather than destroying an adjacency. A
  0.72-viewport loss on recall bought a 1.5-viewport gain on the one element
  that settles "is this real" for a cold-email skeptic. Bigger gain, softer
  loss.

  WhoThisIsFor added 2026-08-23 as the third stop. It sits AFTER the problem
  and BEFORE the proof video on purpose: the reader has just been shown their
  own week, the next question is whether the tool is aimed at someone like
  them, and that has to be settled before the footage plays or the proof lands
  on a reader unsure it is theirs. It costs SeeItRun some depth on the page,
  which is the same currency the measurement above spends; it is worth it
  because the misfit list is the section a cold-email skeptic trusts most, and
  a reader who has decided the page is not about them never reaches the video
  at all. It reuses the /compare no rows rather than making new claims (RC-44).

  If CustomerOutcomes is ever rewritten so its answers stop mirroring
  TheProblem's pains one for one, this trade is void and the order is worth
  re-measuring.

  TheYield returns to the page after sitting unimported. It carries the one
  argument nothing else here makes and no competitor makes either.

  PaperIn added 2026-08-23, eighth stop, sitting seventh. The page argued the
  loop and never said how prices get INTO it, which is the single capability
  meez and Parsley lead with and we had been silent about. It is placed after
  TheYield rather than earlier on purpose: both are mechanics under the loop,
  and putting an import section in front of the proof video would open the page
  on data entry instead of on the job. It is NOT a fifth CustomerOutcomes card,
  because that pairing has to stay 1:1 with TheProblem or the measurement above
  is void. Backed by RC-09 and RC-26; see the header of the component.

  ONE TREATMENT FOR THE SECTION-OPENING ROLE, 2026-08-26. Five of the nine
  stops opened straight on an h2 while three carried an amber eyebrow above it
  (WhoThisIsFor, TheYield, PaperIn). Same semantic role, two costumes, which is
  part of what a reader reported as "a lot of text". The eyebrow is now on all
  nine. Hero keeps its own kicker instead: page-opening is a different role from
  section-opening. The eyebrow is the cheapest device on the page - 31px and two
  or three words per section, no image, no new colour.

  WHAT THE MEASUREMENT SAID. Rendered at 390x844 and 1440x900 with every
  [data-reveal] forced revealed and reduced motion on, the page was NOT short of
  space. Outer isolation - a section's largest internal gap against the gap that
  follows it - ran 1.14 to 8.35 at 390, and the flat-reading stops were at the
  LOW end. Adding air was the one fix the numbers ruled out.

  What they pointed at instead was internal flatness. PaperIn held both records
  at once: the highest isolation on the page (8.35 at 390, 14.4 at 1440) and the
  lowest internal differentiation on it, a 20px largest internal gap. That is
  what produced the longest run of pixels holding no image and no rule at either
  width - 990px at 390 and 824px at 1440, both inside it. A section can be
  perfectly separated from its neighbours and still read as a wall.

  So the fix there was four ticket rules under the four doors, not another 2,000
  pixels of white space. After: 742px at 390, 682px at 1440 (which now falls in
  TheProblem, not here), isolation down to 2.91 and 5.05, page height up 4.3%
  and 3.3%. The full record is BRIEF-visual-density-2026-08-26.md section 5.

  VISUAL GUIDANCE PASS, 2026-08-29. The page had one picture in ten sections
  and nothing pointing down. Seven changes, none of them a new claim: the
  product on the first screen (Hero), the empty right column filled in
  TheProblem, WhoThisIsFor and TheOtherTools, a looked-up hand-off line at the
  foot of every stop (SectionHandoff over src/lib/stops.ts), four footage crops
  in CustomerOutcomes (RC-48), a phone-only bar carrying the one primary
  between the demo and the close (StickyCta), and scroll reveals limited to
  the first three viewports so capture tools stop seeing a blank page.
  Story: docs/stories/homepage-visual-guidance.story.md

  SAGE ADDED 2026-08-29 as the eighth stop, between PaperIn and TheOtherTools,
  at the owner's request. It is a mechanic, so it sits with the mechanics, and
  it precedes the rival beat because that beat's /compare link is where "an
  assistant" appears as a Coming row. It prints its status (src/lib/sage.ts)
  rather than implying one; RC-46 and RC-49 hold the conditions. Not a
  CustomerOutcomes card, for the 1:1 reason above. Story: docs/stories/sage.story.md

  NUTRITION FACTS ADDED 2026-08-29 as the seventh stop, after TheYield and
  before PaperIn, at the owner's request. Both are recipe-level mechanics and
  yield sets up the sentence this one continues (the plate's numbers come from
  the line). Shipped, so no badge; the print page moved "printed labels" from
  Coming to Yes the same day (RC-50). Story: docs/stories/nutrition-facts.story.md
-->
<Base>
	<Hero />
	<TheProblem />
	<WhoThisIsFor />
	<SeeItRun />
	<CustomerOutcomes />
	<TheYield />
	<NutritionFacts />
	<PaperIn />
	<Sage />
	<TheOtherTools />
	<BuiltForKitchens />
	<StartHere />
	<StickyCta />
</Base>
